{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"For a US ’40 Act ETF, liquidation normally means your shares are canceled and you receive your proportionate share of **remaining net assets**, usually as cash through your broker—not automatically a total loss.\n\n- **Amount:** Proceeds reflect realized asset values, less liabilities and liquidation expenses. They may differ from the last trading price or published NAV and are not guaranteed to equal your original investment. A total loss occurs only if nothing remains for shareholders.\n- **Timing:** Follow the fund’s liquidation notices. Trading cessation, asset sales and payments need not coincide; delays or additional payments are possible. There is no universal timetable.\n- **Taxable accounts:** Liquidation generally produces a capital gain or loss versus adjusted tax basis, with holding period determining short- or long-term treatment. Separate income or capital-gain distributions may also be taxable.\n\n**Worth knowing:** Fund closure itself does not mean its underlying assets became worthless."}}
